:: Prototype Workbench Result [activity] {"position":"2525,1800","size":"100,100"}
/* The end of a session at the workbench ("Prototype Workbench"). The minigame leaves its result in
   $workbenchResult = { score 0-1, gain, capped, flawless }; this passage applies the progress, spends the
   session's actions (setup.workbench.actions) and forgets the result again. */
<<if !$workbenchResult>>
	<<goto "Company">>
<<else>>
<<silently>>
	<<set _wbR to $workbenchResult>>
	<<set _wbActions to setup.workbench.actions>>
	<<if _wbR.gain <= 0>><<set _wbMood to "none">>
	<<elseif _wbR.flawless>><<set _wbMood to "flawless">>
	<<elseif _wbR.score >= 0.65>><<set _wbMood to "good">>
	<<elseif _wbR.score >= 0.35>><<set _wbMood to "mid">>
	<<else>><<set _wbMood to "bad">><</if>>
	<<set _wbPick to random(0, 1)>>
<</silently>>
<<Video "images/characters/player/activities/work/2.mp4" "" "" 700>>

<<switch _wbMood + _wbPick>>
<<case "flawless0">>
I lean back, and the chair lets out its usual complaint. Every joint on the board is clean and every test comes back green, which means for once the loudest thing in this room is the fan on my laptop and not me swearing at it. $company.prototype.name sits on the desk looking like something a real company would sell. If I play this right, that's exactly what it's going to be.
<<case "flawless1">>
By the time I put the iron back in its stand, my coffee has gone cold and I haven't touched it once. I don't mind. The board boots on the first try, which has happened maybe twice in the whole history of this project, and the readout on the screen is so steady it almost looks fake. I take a picture of it. Nobody's ever going to see it, but I take one anyway.
<<case "good0">>
It isn't pretty, but it works. There's one joint I'd redo if I had the patience, and one test that only passed after I glared at it for a while, but the board boots and the readout holds steady when I wave a hand in front of the sensor. That's further than it was this morning.
<<case "good1">>
I walk away with a small burn on my thumb and a lot more of $company.prototype.name working than when I sat down. Fair trade. I scribble the two things I fudged on a sticky note and slap it on the lid of the laptop, where future me can deal with them.
<<case "mid0">>
Half of what I touched today works better, and the other half I'd rather not talk about. I clean up the bench anyway, coil the cables and put the solder back in the drawer, because pretending to be organised is most of being organised. The bar moves a little. On a day like this, I'll take a little.
<<case "mid1">>
The test rig and I reach an understanding somewhere along the way: it stops lying to me as long as I stop yelling at it. Some of what I did today will hold. Some of it I'll be ripping out next week, and I can already tell which part.
<<case "bad0">>
Most of this session goes into finding new ways for the board to hate me. A lead I was sure about sparks, the laptop throws an error I've never seen before, and the one thing that does work, I couldn't tell you why. I save what I can and close the lid before I make it worse.
<<case "bad1">>
I stare at the board for a while after the last test fails. It stares back, the way only something held together with hot glue and optimism can. A couple of things are better than they were when I sat down. I decide to focus on those.
<<case "none0">>
A couple of hours, and the only thing I've managed to build is a headache. I undo the worst of it so tomorrow's version of me doesn't have to, and pack the board back into its box.
<<case "none1">>
The board boots exactly as badly as it did when I sat down. The laptop is hot enough to fry an egg on, I smell faintly of burnt flux, and I have nothing to show for any of it. I unplug everything and decide this session never happened.
<</switch>>

<<if _wbR.gain > 0>><<UpdateStat "+" "Prototype" _wbR.gain>><</if>>
<<if _wbR.capped>>
That's about as far as the board goes for now. Whatever it needs next, I won't find it with a soldering iron.
<</if>>
<<UseAction _wbActions>>
<<unset $workbenchResult>>
[[Pack up|Company]]
<</if>>
